window.__BOTFAQ__=[{"id":"q000","g":"Getting started","sub":"When to seek help","q":"We've been trying for a year and nothing. When should we see a fertility doctor?","a":"If you're under 35 and have tried for 12 months with regular unprotected intercourse, it's time to evaluate. If you're 35+ or have irregular cycles, endometriosis, previous pelvic infection, or male issues, come earlier-after 6 months or even sooner. Early assessment saves time and stress."},{"id":"q001","g":"Getting started","sub":"Workup","q":"What tests will you do first for both of us?","a":"We usually start with a simple, step-by-step workup: for her-ultrasound to check uterus/ovaries, AMH/AFC for reserve, thyroid and prolactin, and a tube test (HSG/HyCoSy) if needed; for him-semen analysis and sometimes hormones. Then we tailor next steps based on results."},{"id":"q002","g":"Getting started","sub":"Regular intercourse","q":"How often should we have sex to conceive?","a":"Every 2-3 days through the cycle works for most couples. If cycles are regular, having intercourse on alternate days in the fertile window (about 5 days before ovulation and the day of ovulation) is ideal. Avoid turning it into a strict schedule that adds pressure."},{"id":"q003","g":"Getting started","sub":"Infertility definition","q":"People say we are 'infertile.' Does that mean we can't have a baby naturally?","a":"Not necessarily. Infertility just means pregnancy hasn't happened yet after a certain time. Many causes are treatable, and some couples conceive naturally after guidance. We focus on your specific reason and timeline, not a label."},{"id":"q004","g":"Ovulation and cycle problems","sub":"Ovulation","q":"My periods are irregular. Does that mean I'm not ovulating?","a":"Irregular cycles often signal irregular ovulation, but not always zero ovulation. We confirm with ultrasound tracking or blood tests. If ovulation is inconsistent, medicines and lifestyle changes can help restore it."},{"id":"q005","g":"Ovulation and cycle problems","sub":"PCOS","q":"I have PCOS. Will I definitely need IVF?","a":"Most women with PCOS don't need IVF right away. First-line treatment is weight/lifestyle management, ovulation induction tablets/injections, and timed intercourse or IUI. IVF is considered if these don't work or if there are additional factors."},{"id":"q006","g":"Endometriosis","sub":"Endometriosis","q":"Does endometriosis always cause infertility?","a":"Endometriosis can reduce fertility, but many women still conceive naturally. Severity, age, and ovarian reserve matter. We may use medicines, surgery, or assisted reproduction depending on your stage and symptoms."},{"id":"q007","g":"Uterus, fibroids and polyps","sub":"Adenomyosis/fibroids","q":"I was told I have fibroids/adenomyosis. Can I still get pregnant?","a":"Many fibroids don't affect fertility. Ones that distort the cavity or are large may reduce implantation. Adenomyosis can also affect implantation. We assess size/location and decide if treatment before trying is needed."},{"id":"q008","g":"Ovarian reserve and AMH","sub":"Low AMH","q":"My AMH is low. Is my egg quality poor?","a":"Low AMH mainly indicates lower egg quantity, not automatically poor quality. Quality is more linked to age. With low AMH, we plan efficiently, sometimes aiming for earlier treatment or multiple retrievals if IVF is needed."},{"id":"q009","g":"Age and fertility","sub":"Age","q":"I'm 39. Are my chances too low?","a":"Chances decline with age, but many 39-year-olds conceive-naturally or with help. The key is not to delay evaluation and to choose the right plan (ovulation support, IUI, or IVF depending on results)."},{"id":"q010","g":"Tubes and pelvic factors","sub":"Tubes","q":"If my tubes are blocked, is IVF the only option?","a":"If both tubes are significantly blocked or damaged, IVF is usually the best option because it bypasses the tubes. If one tube is open and healthy, natural/IUI may still be possible depending on other factors."},{"id":"q011","g":"Repeated failure and miscarriage","sub":"Miscarriages","q":"I had two miscarriages. Is IVF with genetic testing needed?","a":"Not automatically. We first look for causes-uterine factors, thyroid/diabetes, antiphospholipid antibodies, chromosomal issues, sperm DNA issues, etc. IVF with PGT may help in selected cases, especially with age 38+ or proven genetic problems."},{"id":"q012","g":"Embryo transfer and freezing","sub":"Luteal phase","q":"My progesterone was low last time. Does that stop pregnancy?","a":"Low progesterone can reduce implantation or early support. If confirmed, we supplement after ovulation or embryo transfer. It's a common, manageable issue."},{"id":"q013","g":"Uterus, fibroids and polyps","sub":"Thin lining","q":"My lining is thin. Can implantation still happen?","a":"Thin lining can reduce chances, but we often improve it with tailored medication, correcting infections, optimizing hormones, and sometimes changing protocol or doing frozen transfer."},{"id":"q014","g":"Ovarian reserve and AMH","sub":"Preserving fertility","q":"Should I freeze eggs now even though I'm not ready for a baby?","a":"Egg freezing is a way to protect future choice, especially if you're in your early-mid 30s or have risk factors (family history, low reserve). We'll check your reserve and discuss expected egg numbers and costs."},{"id":"q015","g":"Semen testing and sperm DNA","sub":"Semen test","q":"What exactly is a semen analysis and how do I prepare?","a":"It measures sperm count, motility (movement), and morphology (shape). You give a sample after 2-5 days of abstinence, avoid fever/alcohol/heavy smoking beforehand, and ideally repeat if abnormal, since sperm changes over time."},{"id":"q016","g":"Semen testing and sperm DNA","sub":"Abnormal semen","q":"My semen report is borderline low. Does that mean I'm infertile?","a":"Borderline values don't equal infertility. Many men with mild issues conceive naturally. We repeat the test, look for treatable causes, and decide if lifestyle, medication, IUI, or IVF/ICSI is best."},{"id":"q017","g":"Male fertility","sub":"Azoospermia","q":"If there is no sperm, is there any hope?","a":"Yes. We first confirm with a repeat test and hormones. Some cases are obstructive and can be treated surgically or with sperm retrieval. Non-obstructive cases may still yield sperm on micro-TESE. Donor sperm is an option if retrieval fails."},{"id":"q018","g":"Male fertility","sub":"Varicocele","q":"I have varicocele. Should I operate?","a":"Varicocele repair can improve semen in selected men-especially if there's pain, a palpable varicocele, and abnormal semen. It may improve natural or IUI chances, and sometimes IVF outcomes."},{"id":"q019","g":"Male fertility","sub":"ED/Timing stress","q":"Performance anxiety is making timing hard. What can we do?","a":"This is common. We can reduce pressure by focusing on regular intercourse rather than a single day, using ovulation tracking calmly, and involving counseling. If needed, a short course of medication or IUI can remove timing stress."},{"id":"q020","g":"Male fertility","sub":"Age effect","q":"Does the man's age matter for fertility?","a":"Yes, sperm quality and DNA integrity gradually decline with age, and miscarriage risk can rise in older fathers. Healthy lifestyle and timely planning help, and in IVF we may consider sperm DNA testing in some cases."},{"id":"q021","g":"Lifestyle, diet and supplements","sub":"Lifestyle","q":"What lifestyle changes improve sperm?","a":"Stop smoking/vaping, limit alcohol, maintain healthy weight, exercise moderately, sleep 7-8 hours, reduce heat exposure to testes, and eat a balanced diet with enough protein, omega-3, zinc, folate, and antioxidants."},{"id":"q022","g":"Semen testing and sperm DNA","sub":"DNA fragmentation","q":"What is sperm DNA fragmentation and when do you test it?","a":"It checks sperm DNA breaks that can affect fertilization and miscarriage. We consider it after repeated IVF failure, unexplained infertility, recurrent miscarriage, or in older males/varicocele."},{"id":"q023","g":"Male fertility","sub":"Infections","q":"Can past infections cause male infertility?","a":"Yes, infections like mumps orchitis, STIs, or prostatitis can affect sperm. We look for symptoms, do tests if needed, and treat infections to improve outcomes."},{"id":"q024","g":"Male fertility","sub":"Medication impact","q":"Do diabetes or BP medicines affect fertility?","a":"Some illnesses and a few medicines can affect erectile function or sperm. We review your drugs and control sugars/BP well. Never stop medicines on your own-let's adjust safely if needed."},{"id":"q025","g":"Getting started","sub":"Unexplained","q":"All tests are normal. Why aren't we conceiving?","a":"This is called unexplained infertility. It may involve subtle egg/sperm/implantation issues we can't measure yet. Options include timed intercourse with mild stimulation, IUI, and IVF depending on age and duration."},{"id":"q026","g":"Getting started","sub":"Next steps","q":"How do you decide between IUI and IVF?","a":"We use age, duration, tube status, sperm quality, ovarian reserve, and previous attempts. IUI suits younger couples with open tubes and mild male/ovulation issues; IVF is better for low reserve, tubal block, severe male factor, or long-standing infertility."},{"id":"q027","g":"Planning IVF","sub":"Overview","q":"Can you explain IVF in simple steps?","a":"Sure: 1) stimulate ovaries with injections, 2) monitor follicles, 3) retrieve eggs under anesthesia, 4) fertilize eggs with sperm in lab (IVF/ICSI), 5) grow embryos 3-5 days, 6) transfer one embryo, and 7) support hormones and do a pregnancy test."},{"id":"q028","g":"Planning IVF","sub":"Duration","q":"How long does one IVF cycle take?","a":"Usually 3-4 weeks from start of injections to embryo transfer. If we freeze embryos and transfer later, each step is still within a month or so, but the full journey may span 1-3 months."},{"id":"q029","g":"Egg retrieval and safety","sub":"Pain","q":"Is egg retrieval painful or risky?","a":"Retrieval is done under short anesthesia, so you shouldn't feel pain during it. Mild cramps/bloating after are common for 1-3 days. Serious risks like bleeding/infection are rare."},{"id":"q030","g":"In the laboratory","sub":"ICSI","q":"What is ICSI and when is it needed?","a":"ICSI means injecting a single sperm into each egg. We use it for low count/motility, azoospermia with retrieved sperm, previous fertilization failure, or sometimes for older couples."},{"id":"q031","g":"Embryo transfer and freezing","sub":"Fresh vs frozen","q":"What's the difference between fresh and frozen embryo transfer?","a":"Fresh transfer happens in the same cycle as retrieval. Frozen transfer uses embryos stored earlier and transferred in a later cycle when the uterus is best prepared. Frozen transfers can reduce OHSS risk and sometimes improve implantation."},{"id":"q032","g":"Planning IVF","sub":"Success rates by age","q":"What are realistic IVF success chances at my age?","a":"Success depends mainly on female age and embryo quality. Rough guide for live birth per transfer using own eggs: about 35-45% under 35, ~25-35% at 35-37, ~15-25% at 38-40, ~5-10% at 41-42, and <5% over 42. Your personal chances may be higher or lower based on diagnosis."},{"id":"q033","g":"Planning IVF","sub":"Number of cycles","q":"How many IVF cycles do people usually need?","a":"Many couples conceive in 1-3 cycles. Because biology is variable, we plan in batches and review after each attempt rather than promising a fixed number."},{"id":"q034","g":"In the laboratory","sub":"Embryo grading","q":"What does embryo grading mean?","a":"It's a lab-based way of describing how embryos look and develop. Higher-graded embryos generally have better chances, but even average-graded embryos can result in healthy babies."},{"id":"q035","g":"Embryo transfer and freezing","sub":"Single embryo transfer","q":"Why are you advising only one embryo transfer?","a":"Single embryo transfer lowers twin/triplet risks without reducing overall success much, especially in women under 38 with good embryos. If needed, we can transfer remaining frozen embryos later."},{"id":"q036","g":"Egg retrieval and safety","sub":"OHSS worry","q":"I heard about OHSS. How common and how do you prevent it?","a":"OHSS is ovarian over-response causing bloating, fluid shifts, and rarely clots. Severe OHSS is uncommon, and we prevent it by adjusting doses, using antagonist protocols, triggering safely, and freezing all embryos if risk is high."},{"id":"q037","g":"Repeated failure and miscarriage","sub":"Failed cycle","q":"What if IVF fails-does that mean no chance?","a":"Not at all. Failure gives us information. We review embryo quality, uterine factors, sperm DNA, and protocol, then modify the next plan. Many couples succeed on a later attempt."},{"id":"q038","g":"Stimulation and monitoring","sub":"Cancelled cycle","q":"Why would a cycle get cancelled?","a":"Reasons include too few follicles, over-response risk, premature ovulation, or medical issues. Cancellation is frustrating but sometimes safer than proceeding with low chances."},{"id":"q039","g":"Genetics and PGT","sub":"PGT-A","q":"What is PGT-A and who should consider it?","a":"PGT-A checks embryos for chromosome number errors to select a euploid embryo. It's most helpful for women 38+, recurrent miscarriage, repeated IVF failure, or severe male factor/older paternal age."},{"id":"q040","g":"Genetics and PGT","sub":"PGT-M","q":"We have a known genetic disease in family. Can IVF prevent passing it?","a":"Yes. With PGT-M, we test embryos for a specific gene mutation and transfer only unaffected embryos. It needs genetic counseling and a custom test setup."},{"id":"q041","g":"Genetics and PGT","sub":"Carrier screening","q":"Should we do genetic carrier screening before IVF?","a":"Carrier screening can identify hidden recessive disease risks. It's optional but useful if there's family history, consanguinity, or you want maximum reassurance before treatment."},{"id":"q042","g":"Genetics and PGT","sub":"Thalassemia/India","q":"We are thalassemia carriers. What are our options?","a":"You can conceive naturally with prenatal diagnosis, or do IVF with PGT-M to transfer unaffected embryos. The choice depends on your values, cost, and timeline."},{"id":"q043","g":"Genetics and PGT","sub":"Age-related anomalies","q":"Does IVF reduce the chance of Down syndrome?","a":"IVF itself doesn't reduce risk, but selecting embryos via PGT-A or doing prenatal screening in pregnancy can lower the chance of transferring an aneuploid embryo."},{"id":"q044","g":"Pregnancy after IVF","sub":"Post-transfer care","q":"After embryo transfer, what precautions should I take?","a":"Think normal life with moderation: avoid heavy lifting, intense workouts, smoking/alcohol, and overheating. Continue medicines, eat balanced meals, and try to stay calm. Bed rest isn't required."},{"id":"q045","g":"Pregnancy after IVF","sub":"Symptoms","q":"I have no symptoms after transfer. Is that bad?","a":"No. Some women feel nothing and still get positive results. Symptoms depend more on hormones than on pregnancy itself."},{"id":"q046","g":"Pregnancy after IVF","sub":"Bleeding","q":"Light spotting after IVF-should I panic?","a":"Light spotting can happen from implantation or cervical irritation. If bleeding is heavy, painful, or persistent, contact us immediately. We'll guide you."},{"id":"q047","g":"Pregnancy after IVF","sub":"Ectopic risk","q":"Is ectopic pregnancy possible in IVF?","a":"Yes, but still uncommon. IVF slightly increases the risk versus natural conception, so we do early ultrasounds to confirm the pregnancy location."},{"id":"q048","g":"Pregnancy after IVF","sub":"Multiple pregnancy","q":"What if twins happen? Is it dangerous?","a":"Twins can be healthy but carry higher risks: preterm birth, diabetes, hypertension, and NICU admission. That's why we prefer single embryo transfer whenever possible."},{"id":"q049","g":"Pregnancy after IVF","sub":"ANC after IVF","q":"Do IVF pregnancies need special monitoring?","a":"Most follow standard antenatal care, with a bit more early monitoring. If there are additional risks (age, twins, diabetes, OHSS), we add targeted scans/consults."},{"id":"q050","g":"Pregnancy after IVF","sub":"C-section","q":"Is C-section mandatory after IVF?","a":"No. Mode of delivery depends on obstetric indications, not IVF alone. Many IVF mothers deliver vaginally."},{"id":"q051","g":"Pregnancy after IVF","sub":"Success after heartbeat","q":"Once heartbeat is seen, what are chances of continuing?","a":"A confirmed fetal heartbeat at 6-7 weeks is a strong positive sign. Miscarriage risk drops significantly after this, though exact risk depends on age and past history."},{"id":"q052","g":"Pregnancy after IVF","sub":"Baby health","q":"Are IVF babies normal? Any long-term risks?","a":"Most IVF children are healthy and develop normally. Some risks seen in studies relate more to underlying infertility, multiple pregnancy, or prematurity than to IVF itself."},{"id":"q053","g":"Pregnancy after IVF","sub":"Birth defects","q":"Is the risk of birth defects higher with IVF/ICSI?","a":"Overall risk is only slightly higher and still low. Part of the increase is linked to parental factors (age, genetics, male factor) rather than the technique itself."},{"id":"q054","g":"Egg retrieval and safety","sub":"Cancer myth","q":"Does IVF increase cancer risk in women?","a":"Current evidence does not show a meaningful increase in breast/ovarian/uterine cancer from IVF medicines. We still individualize protocols for women with specific risks."},{"id":"q055","g":"Feelings and support","sub":"Mental health","q":"I'm feeling anxious and irritable on injections. Is this normal?","a":"Yes, hormones plus uncertainty can affect mood. Tell us early. We can support you medically and with counseling, and adjust expectations and coping tools."},{"id":"q056","g":"Costs and payment","sub":"India basic IVF","q":"What is a typical IVF cost per cycle in India?","a":"In India, a basic IVF cycle often ranges roughly \u20b91.2-2.5 lakh, depending on city, clinic, medicines, and complexity. Add-ons like ICSI, freezing, or genetic testing increase cost. We'll give a transparent written estimate for your case."},{"id":"q057","g":"Costs and payment","sub":"ICSI cost","q":"How much extra does ICSI cost?","a":"ICSI is usually an add-on cost on top of IVF. In many Indian centers it can add roughly \u20b915,000-\u20b940,000 or more depending on lab and case; we'll confirm your exact package."},{"id":"q058","g":"Costs and payment","sub":"PGT cost","q":"How much does PGT cost?","a":"PGT can add about \u20b91-3 lakh+ depending on number of embryos and type (PGT-A vs PGT-M). We'll explain benefits so you decide if it's worth it for you."},{"id":"q059","g":"Costs and payment","sub":"Donor eggs","q":"If I need donor eggs, what does it cost and what are success rates?","a":"Donor-egg IVF costs more because of the screening, the donor's medical care and insurance, and the bank's charges; many Indian clinics quote several lakhs per cycle. Under the ART (Regulation) Act 2021 no payment may be made for the eggs themselves, and donors are recruited through a registered ART bank rather than by the clinic. Success per transfer is generally higher than with very low-reserve own eggs."},{"id":"q060","g":"Costs and payment","sub":"Payment plans","q":"Do you offer EMI or packages?","a":"Many clinics offer package deals or EMI through partners. We can discuss options so finances don't force rushed decisions."},{"id":"q061","g":"Costs and payment","sub":"Hidden charges","q":"I'm scared of hidden charges. How do I avoid surprises?","a":"Ask for an itemized estimate including medicines, anesthesia, lab procedures, freezing, and transfers. Clarify what is included/excluded before starting. A good clinic will be transparent."},{"id":"q062","g":"Lifestyle, diet and supplements","sub":"Diet female","q":"What diet helps female fertility?","a":"A balanced, anti-inflammatory diet helps: plenty of vegetables, fruits, whole grains, pulses, nuts, healthy fats, and adequate protein. Limit excess sugar, trans-fats, and ultra-processed foods."},{"id":"q063","g":"Lifestyle, diet and supplements","sub":"Diet male","q":"Any specific foods for sperm health?","a":"Focus on protein, omega-3 sources, nuts/seeds, leafy greens, colorful fruits, and adequate micronutrients like zinc, selenium, folate, and vitamin D. Consistency matters more than one 'superfood'."},{"id":"q064","g":"Lifestyle, diet and supplements","sub":"Weight","q":"Does weight really affect fertility?","a":"Yes. Both underweight and overweight can disturb hormones, ovulation, and sperm. Even a 5-10% weight correction can improve natural and assisted pregnancy rates."},{"id":"q065","g":"Lifestyle, diet and supplements","sub":"Exercise","q":"Can I exercise during IVF?","a":"Light to moderate activity is fine. Avoid high-impact workouts and heavy core exercises during stimulation and after transfer to reduce torsion/discomfort."},{"id":"q066","g":"Lifestyle, diet and supplements","sub":"Alcohol/caffeine","q":"Is coffee or alcohol completely banned?","a":"Moderation. Limit caffeine to about 1-2 cups a day and avoid binge drinking. In the 2-week wait and pregnancy, it's safest to avoid alcohol."},{"id":"q067","g":"Lifestyle, diet and supplements","sub":"Smoking","q":"How much does smoking affect fertility?","a":"Smoking reduces egg reserve, impairs embryo quality, and lowers sperm count/motility. Quitting improves outcomes within a few months."},{"id":"q068","g":"Lifestyle, diet and supplements","sub":"Supplements","q":"Which supplements should we take?","a":"Common basics: folic acid for women, vitamin D if deficient, and antioxidants for men if semen is poor. But supplements should be tailored to your labs-avoid megadoses without advice."},{"id":"q069","g":"Feelings and support","sub":"Stress cause","q":"Did stress cause our infertility?","a":"Stress alone rarely causes infertility, but it can worsen hormones, libido, and coping. Think of stress as a load on top of a medical issue, not the only reason."},{"id":"q070","g":"Feelings and support","sub":"Coping","q":"How do we handle the two-week wait emotionally?","a":"Keep a simple routine, avoid constant symptom-checking, limit social media triggers, and share feelings with your partner or counselor. We're here to support you too."},{"id":"q071","g":"Feelings and support","sub":"Partner conflict","q":"We keep blaming each other. How do we stop?","a":"Infertility is a couple's condition, not a person's fault. Try using 'we' language, attend counseling together, and focus on teamwork and timelines."},{"id":"q072","g":"Feelings and support","sub":"Social stigma","q":"Family keeps pressuring us. What should we say?","a":"Set kind but firm boundaries: 'We're under medical care and will share updates when ready.' You don't owe detailed explanations. Protect your mental space."},{"id":"q073","g":"Feelings and support","sub":"Isolation","q":"I feel alone because friends are getting pregnant. Is that normal?","a":"Very normal. Infertility grief is real. Seek a support group or counselor; talking to people who get it can reduce isolation."},{"id":"q074","g":"Feelings and support","sub":"Male stigma","q":"My husband feels ashamed about male factor. How can I help him?","a":"Reassure him that male factor is common and treatable. Avoid sharing his reports with others. Come as a team to visits so he feels supported, not singled out."},{"id":"q075","g":"Law and consent in India","sub":"Ethics India ART law","q":"Is IVF legal and regulated in India?","a":"Yes. IVF is legal and governed by ART and Surrogacy regulations. Clinics must be registered and follow consent, donor screening, and embryo handling rules."},{"id":"q076","g":"Law and consent in India","sub":"Donor anonymity","q":"Will we know the egg/sperm donor?","a":"In India, donors are typically anonymous; you receive medical and phenotypic matching details, not identity. Records are maintained for safety."},{"id":"q077","g":"Law and consent in India","sub":"Embryo storage","q":"How long can embryos be frozen?","a":"Embryos can be safely frozen for years with good survival. The legal storage duration depends on national rules and consent, and we renew your consent periodically."},{"id":"q078","g":"Law and consent in India","sub":"Surrogacy","q":"When is surrogacy advised?","a":"Surrogacy is considered when a woman cannot carry a pregnancy safely (absent uterus, severe medical disease, repeated implantation failure after evaluation). It must follow legal criteria and approvals."},{"id":"q079","g":"Repeated failure and miscarriage","sub":"Loss after IVF","q":"If miscarriage happens after IVF, what next?","a":"We do a structured review-genetics of the pregnancy if possible, uterine evaluation, hormones, clotting and immune factors, and sperm quality. Then plan next transfer with corrections."},{"id":"q080","g":"Pregnancy after IVF","sub":"Breastfeeding","q":"Will IVF hormones affect breastfeeding later?","a":"No lasting effect. The hormones used are temporary and don't stop future breastfeeding."},{"id":"q081","g":"Pregnancy after IVF","sub":"Sex after transfer","q":"Is sex allowed after embryo transfer?","a":"Usually yes after a short rest (often 3-5 days), unless you had bleeding, pain, or high OHSS risk. We'll tell you based on your case."},{"id":"q082","g":"Pregnancy after IVF","sub":"Travel","q":"Can I travel during IVF or early pregnancy?","a":"Light travel is okay during stimulation if monitoring is not missed. After transfer, short trips are fine if you're stable, but avoid very long or stressful travel unless cleared."},{"id":"q083","g":"Planning IVF","sub":"Choosing clinic","q":"How do we choose a good IVF clinic?","a":"Look for transparent data, experienced embryology lab, individualized protocols, clear communication, and ethical counseling-not just flashy success ads. Meeting the doctor and lab team matters."},{"id":"q084","g":"Embryo transfer and freezing","sub":"Embryo freezing decision","q":"Should we freeze embryos for future child?","a":"If you want another child later or if age/medical condition may worsen, freezing good embryos now is smart. It's usually cheaper and more effective than starting over later."},{"id":"q085","g":"Add-ons and donor options","sub":"Adjuvants","q":"Do add-on treatments like PRP, growth hormone, or immunotherapy always help?","a":"Not always. Some add-ons help selected patients, but many don't have strong evidence for routine use. We recommend only when your profile suggests benefit."},{"id":"q086","g":"Planning IVF","sub":"Natural vs IVF","q":"Is it okay to keep trying naturally while planning IVF?","a":"Yes, unless there's a clear barrier like both tubes blocked. We encourage healthy attempts while we prepare, because natural conception can still happen."},{"id":"q087","g":"Ovulation and cycle problems","sub":"Thyroid","q":"My TSH is high-normal. Does that matter?","a":"Thyroid affects ovulation and early pregnancy. We aim for a TSH around 2.5 or less when trying or in early pregnancy, and treat if needed."},{"id":"q088","g":"Ovulation and cycle problems","sub":"Prolactin","q":"My prolactin is a bit elevated. Is that serious?","a":"Mild elevation is common and treatable. We repeat fasting tests, rule out stress/medication causes, and give treatment if needed to restore ovulation."},{"id":"q089","g":"Repeated failure and miscarriage","sub":"Autoimmune","q":"Do autoimmune diseases affect fertility?","a":"Some autoimmune conditions can affect implantation or pregnancy. With proper control and a coordinated plan with your physician, most women conceive safely."},{"id":"q090","g":"Lifestyle, diet and supplements","sub":"Testosterone supplements","q":"Can I take testosterone to improve sperm?","a":"External testosterone can actually suppress sperm production. Never self-start. If hormones are low, we use fertility-friendly treatments instead."},{"id":"q091","g":"Lifestyle, diet and supplements","sub":"Supplements timing","q":"How long do sperm-improving treatments take?","a":"Sperm take about 70-90 days to regenerate. So lifestyle or medication changes usually need 3 months to show effect."},{"id":"q092","g":"Male fertility","sub":"Sperm retrieval fear","q":"Is sperm retrieval surgery painful?","a":"Procedures like PESA/TESA are minor and done with anesthesia; discomfort afterward is usually mild. Micro-TESE is a bigger procedure, but pain is manageable and we give proper care."},{"id":"q093","g":"Law and consent in India","sub":"Sex selection myth","q":"Can IVF guarantee a boy/girl?","a":"No. Sex selection for social reasons is illegal in India. IVF is for treating infertility and preventing serious genetic disease."},{"id":"q094","g":"Feelings and support","sub":"Religion","q":"Is IVF acceptable in our religion?","a":"Many couples reconcile IVF with faith by focusing on the intention-building a family. If you want, we can discuss options that align with your beliefs, like using your own gametes."},{"id":"q095","g":"Stimulation and monitoring","sub":"Period delay during IVF","q":"If my period comes late before starting IVF, what do we do?","a":"We confirm you're not pregnant and check hormones/ultrasound. If everything is okay, we can induce a period safely or start in the next natural cycle."},{"id":"q096","g":"Ovarian reserve and AMH","sub":"Oocyte quality","q":"Can egg quality be improved?","a":"We can optimize factors that hurt quality-smoking, obesity, uncontrolled thyroid/diabetes, vitamin D deficiency, oxidative stress. But age-related decline can't be fully reversed, so timing matters."},{"id":"q097","g":"Lifestyle, diet and supplements","sub":"Heat exposure","q":"Does laptop/sauna/hot baths reduce sperm?","a":"Regular heat exposure can lower sperm count temporarily. Keep laptops off lap, limit saunas/hot tubs, and wear loose underwear."},{"id":"q098","g":"Costs and payment","sub":"Insurance","q":"Does insurance cover IVF in India?","a":"Coverage is still limited, though some corporate policies include add-ons. We can provide documents for claims if your policy supports it."},{"id":"q099","g":"Costs and payment","sub":"Refund packages","q":"Are refund-or-guarantee packages safe?","a":"They can be okay if terms are transparent, but read fine print about age, embryo number, exclusions, and what counts as 'success'. Don't choose a clinic only for a guarantee."},{"id":"q100","g":"Getting started","sub":"Overview","q":"What does the first fertility assessment involve?","a":"A fertility plan should be individualized after reviewing age, duration of trying, menstrual history, previous pregnancies/treatments and both partners' reports. Testing should be targeted rather than ordering every test for everyone.","ov":1,"alias":["when to seek help","first visit assessment","ovulation history","partner evaluation","previous treatment records"]},{"id":"q101","g":"Ovulation and cycle problems","sub":"Overview","q":"How do you confirm whether I am ovulating, and what happens if I am not?","a":"The key is to confirm whether and when ovulation occurs and then address the underlying cause. Ultrasound, selected blood tests and cycle tracking can guide treatment; treatment depends on the diagnosis.","ov":1,"alias":["irregular periods","ovulation tracking","late periods","anovulation","ovulation medicines"]},{"id":"q102","g":"Ovarian reserve and AMH","sub":"Overview","q":"What do AMH and AFC actually tell you?","a":"AMH and AFC mainly estimate ovarian reserve/expected response to stimulation; they do not directly measure egg quality. Age remains an important predictor of egg competence and pregnancy outcome.","ov":1,"alias":["AMH meaning","low AMH","high AMH","AFC meaning","ovarian reserve and age"]},{"id":"q103","g":"Age and fertility","sub":"Overview","q":"How does age change our chances, and how should that affect the plan?","a":"Fertility generally declines with increasing age, particularly because egg number and chromosomal competence change. The best plan depends on age, ovarian reserve, sperm factors, duration of trying and the couple's goals.","ov":1,"alias":["fertility after 35","fertility after 40","egg quality with age","male age","timing treatment"]},{"id":"q104","g":"Tubes and pelvic factors","sub":"Overview","q":"When are the tubes tested, and what happens if they are damaged?","a":"Tubal assessment is useful when tubal disease is possible. Treatment depends on whether one or both tubes are affected, whether there is hydrosalpinx or other pelvic disease, and the couple's age and fertility factors.","ov":1,"alias":["HSG/HyCoSy","blocked tubes","hydrosalpinx","one open tube","tubal surgery"]},{"id":"q105","g":"Uterus, fibroids and polyps","sub":"Overview","q":"Which uterine findings actually matter for fertility?","a":"Uterine findings matter mainly when they alter the cavity or are associated with symptoms or repeated reproductive problems. Ultrasound and, when indicated, hysteroscopy or other imaging help decide whether treatment is worthwhile.","ov":1,"alias":["fibroids","endometrial polyps","uterine septum","thin endometrium","adenomyosis"]},{"id":"q106","g":"Endometriosis","sub":"Overview","q":"How does endometriosis affect fertility, and how is it managed?","a":"Endometriosis can affect fertility through inflammation, anatomy and ovarian reserve, but its impact varies widely. Treatment should balance symptoms, age, ovarian reserve, prior surgery and the couple's fertility goals.","ov":1,"alias":["endometriosis diagnosis","endometriosis and egg reserve","endometriosis pain","surgery before IVF","endometriosis recurrence"]},{"id":"q107","g":"Male fertility","sub":"Overview","q":"How is the male partner evaluated?","a":"Male factors contribute to a substantial proportion of infertility. Evaluation usually starts with semen analysis and history, followed by targeted hormonal, genetic or imaging tests when indicated.","ov":1,"alias":["low sperm count","low motility","abnormal morphology","azoospermia","male fertility hormones"]},{"id":"q108","g":"Semen testing and sperm DNA","sub":"Overview","q":"How reliable is a semen report, and when is sperm DNA testing needed?","a":"Semen results vary from sample to sample, so an abnormal report often needs confirmation. Sperm DNA testing is a selected investigation rather than a routine test for every couple, and management should address treatable causes first.","ov":1,"alias":["semen sample preparation","repeat semen analysis","DFI testing","infection and semen","sperm freezing"]},{"id":"q109","g":"Lifestyle, diet and supplements","sub":"Overview","q":"Which lifestyle changes and supplements are worth the effort?","a":"Healthy weight, regular moderate activity, adequate sleep, a balanced diet and avoidance of tobacco and recreational drugs support reproductive health. Supplements should be used selectively because evidence and needs vary between patients.","ov":1,"alias":["weight","exercise","diet","smoking/alcohol","supplements"]},{"id":"q110","g":"IUI","sub":"Overview","q":"Who does IUI actually help?","a":"IUI is most useful in selected couples, particularly when at least one tube is functional and sperm parameters are suitable. Success depends strongly on age, ovarian response, diagnosis and sperm factors.","ov":1,"alias":["who should consider IUI","IUI timing","IUI medicines","IUI success factors","IUI failure"]},{"id":"q111","g":"Planning IVF","sub":"Overview","q":"When is IVF the right choice rather than a simpler treatment?","a":"IVF is considered when the expected benefit is higher than simpler options, such as with significant tubal disease, some male factors, reduced ovarian reserve, prolonged infertility or failed lower-intensity treatment. The plan should be personalized.","ov":1,"alias":["who needs IVF","IVF steps","IVF preparation","number of cycles","choosing protocol"]},{"id":"q112","g":"Stimulation and monitoring","sub":"Overview","q":"What is stimulation trying to achieve, and how is it monitored?","a":"Stimulation aims to recruit multiple follicles while minimizing complications. Medication doses and monitoring are individualized using ultrasound, clinical findings and sometimes hormone levels.","ov":1,"alias":["injections","follicle monitoring","estradiol","trigger injection","cycle cancellation"]},{"id":"q113","g":"Egg retrieval and safety","sub":"Overview","q":"What happens at egg retrieval, and what are the risks?","a":"Egg retrieval is usually performed with analgesia or short anesthesia. Most patients recover quickly, but bleeding, infection, ovarian torsion and OHSS are uncommon complications that should be discussed before treatment.","ov":1,"alias":["egg pickup","anesthesia","retrieval risks","OHSS prevention","after retrieval"]},{"id":"q114","g":"In the laboratory","sub":"Overview","q":"What decides how the embryos do in the laboratory?","a":"Laboratory outcomes depend on egg maturity, sperm quality, fertilization method and embryo biology. ICSI is useful for selected indications but is not automatically better than conventional IVF for every couple.","ov":1,"alias":["IVF vs ICSI","fertilization rate","embryo development","blastocyst","embryo grading"]},{"id":"q115","g":"Embryo transfer and freezing","sub":"Overview","q":"How is the timing of the embryo transfer decided?","a":"Transfer timing is chosen after considering embryo development, endometrial preparation, hormone levels and clinical circumstances. Single embryo transfer is commonly preferred when appropriate to reduce multiple-pregnancy risk.","ov":1,"alias":["fresh transfer","frozen transfer","endometrial preparation","single embryo transfer","transfer precautions"]},{"id":"q116","g":"Pregnancy after IVF","sub":"Overview","q":"What happens in the first weeks after a positive test?","a":"After a positive test, medication and follow-up are continued according to the treatment plan. Serial beta-hCG and ultrasound are used when appropriate to confirm pregnancy location and development.","ov":1,"alias":["beta hCG","early scan","spotting","ectopic pregnancy","multiple pregnancy"]},{"id":"q117","g":"Repeated failure and miscarriage","sub":"Overview","q":"How is repeated failure or recurrent miscarriage investigated?","a":"Repeated failure or miscarriage deserves a structured review rather than automatically adding multiple tests. The evaluation should consider embryo factors, uterine anatomy, endocrine issues, sperm factors and other findings supported by the clinical history.","ov":1,"alias":["repeated implantation failure","recurrent miscarriage","chronic endometritis","uterine reassessment","sperm/embryo factors"]},{"id":"q118","g":"Genetics and PGT","sub":"Overview","q":"When is genetic testing worth doing?","a":"Genetic testing is most useful when there is a clear clinical indication. Carrier screening, PGT-M and PGT-A answer different questions and have limitations; genetic counseling can help couples choose appropriately.","ov":1,"alias":["carrier screening","PGT-A","PGT-M","chromosomal abnormalities","family genetic disease"]},{"id":"q119","g":"Add-ons and donor options","sub":"Overview","q":"How should we decide about add-ons and donor treatment?","a":"Optional treatments should be offered only when their potential benefit, evidence, risks and costs are clear. Donor treatment, embryo storage, consent and finances should be discussed transparently and in accordance with applicable Indian regulations.","ov":1,"alias":["PRP/GH/add-ons","donor eggs","donor sperm","embryo freezing","cost/consent/emotional support"]},{"id":"q120","g":"Getting started","sub":"How decisions are made","q":"How do you decide what to do next, and when should we come sooner?","a":"The doctor usually decides the next step after reviewing the relevant reports rather than from one number alone, and any single finding is read in the context of your age, your history and the other results. A result on its own does not mean that pregnancy is impossible; what matters is the complete clinical picture. Bring previous reports and a list of medicines to the consultation, and do not start or stop treatment without medical advice. Come sooner than the usual timelines if there are red flags - very irregular cycles, severe pelvic pain, repeated pregnancy loss, or a significant semen abnormality.","ov":1},{"id":"q121","g":"Appointments and the clinic","sub":"Appointments","q":"How do I book an appointment with Dr Gupta?","a":"Call or WhatsApp +91 98999 84791 - the same number takes both. Consultations at Indira IVF, J.P. Nagar, Bengaluru are by appointment. If you are travelling from outside Bengaluru or from abroad, ask for a video consultation first and send your reports in advance."},{"id":"q122","g":"Appointments and the clinic","sub":"Location","q":"Where is the clinic?","a":"Indira IVF, Woody's Building, 3rd Floor, Plot No. 45/1-1, near Bangalore Central Mall, J.P. Nagar, Bengaluru 560078, Karnataka. It is convenient for Jayanagar, Banashankari, BTM Layout and Bannerghatta Road."},{"id":"q123","g":"Appointments and the clinic","sub":"First visit","q":"What should I bring to the first visit?","a":"Every report you already have: previous cycle summaries and discharge notes, semen analyses, hormone profiles including AMH, thyroid and prolactin, HSG or ultrasound reports, hysteroscopy or laparoscopy notes, and the medicines used in earlier cycles. A consultation with the old records in the room is far more useful than one starting from scratch."},{"id":"q124","g":"Appointments and the clinic","sub":"Second opinion","q":"Can I come for a second opinion after treatment elsewhere?","a":"Yes, and that is a large part of the practice. Bring the records from the previous centre. The point of the first visit is to work out what happened and why, before anything new is planned."},{"id":"q125","g":"Appointments and the clinic","sub":"Languages","q":"Which languages can we consult in?","a":"English, Hindi, Nepali and Bhojpuri, and conversational Kannada and Bengali. The website itself can be read in Kannada, Bengali and Nepali using the language buttons at the top of the page."},{"id":"q126","g":"Appointments and the clinic","sub":"About the doctor","q":"Who is Dr Shyam N Gupta?","a":"MBBS, MD in Obstetrics and Gynaecology, with fellowships in infertility and gynaecological endoscopy and a diploma in male infertility from ANDROFERT, Brazil. Centre Head at Indira IVF, J.P. Nagar, Bengaluru since 2016 and Zonal Business Director for Indira IVF South, with sixteen publications and twelve years in reproductive medicine. Much of the practice concerns couples for whom something has already gone wrong more than once."}];
